.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt GETTIMEOFDAY 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gettimeofday ,
.Nm settimeofday ,
.Nm clock_gettime ,
.Nm clock_settime ,
.Nm clock_getres ,
.Nm stime ,
.Nm time ,
.Nm times ,
.Nm utimensat
.Nd read and set clocks, and set file timestamps
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/time.h
.Ft int
.Fn gettimeofday "struct timeval *restrict tv" "struct timezone *restrict tz"
.Ft int
.Fn settimeofday "const struct timeval *tv" "const struct timezone *tz"
.In time.h
.Ft int
.Fn clock_gettime "clockid_t clockid" "struct timespec *tp"
.Ft int
.Fn clock_settime "clockid_t clockid" "const struct timespec *tp"
.Ft int
.Fn stime "const time_t *t"
.Ft time_t
.Fn time "time_t *tloc"
.Ft int
.Fn clock_getres "clockid_t clockid" "struct timespec *res"
.In sys/times.h
.Ft clock_t
.Fn times "struct tms *buf"
.In sys/stat.h
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn utimensat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "const struct timespec times[2]" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn gettimeofday
reports real time in
.Fa tv ,
as seconds and microseconds, and the time zone that
.Fn settimeofday
last set in
.Fa tz ,
minutes west of UTC and the kind of summer time, both 0 until one is set;
either may be null.
It writes
.Fa tv
first, so that a
.Fa tv
that cannot be written fails the call with
.Fa tz
unwritten, for an i386 program too.
No clock reads the zone; a vfat mount without
.Ql tz=
or
.Ql time_offset= ,
and an exfat mount with
.Ql sys_tz ,
read and write their stamps in it, as on Linux; see
.Xr mount 2 .
Real time is the CMOS clock reading taken at boot plus
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
the time since, to the microsecond, so that the rate that
.Xr adjtimex 2
gives the monotonic clock, by which an NTP daemon slews it, moves real time
too.
If the clock could not be read, the result is time since boot.
The hardware clock is read once.
.Fn time
returns the seconds of real time at the last tick, and stores them at
.Fa tloc
as well when it is not null.
.Pp
.Fn clock_settime
of
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
.Fn settimeofday
and
.Fn stime
set the real time, for a privileged caller alone.
The difference from the time the clock keeps becomes a step that every
later reading of real time adds, so the clock goes on from the new time at
the same rate, and a reading after a step back may be earlier than one
before it.
A setting forgets the offset that
.Xr adjtimex 2
was slewing, and keeps its frequency.
The boot time that
.Pa /proc/stat
gives moves with the step, as on Linux.
The hardware clock is not written, so the next boot reads the CMOS again.
.Fn settimeofday
also sets the time zone from a
.Fa tz
that is not null, for a privileged caller alone and from \-900 to 900
minutes, else
.Er EINVAL ;
either argument may be null, and a call with neither checks only the
privilege.
The first zone set since boot, when it comes without
.Fa tv
and is not UTC, moves real time on by its minutes west, as Linux does
for a hardware clock kept in local time, which is how
.Xr hwclock 1
tells the kernel of one.
Their i386 system call numbers are 264, with a 64-bit timespec 404, for
.Fn clock_settime ,
79 for
.Fn settimeofday
and 25 for
.Fn stime .
.Pp
.Fn clock_gettime
stores seconds and nanoseconds in
.Fa tp .
The clocks are:
.Bl -tag -width "CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID"
.It Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
ID 0.
The same real time reported by
.Fn gettimeofday .
.It Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
ID 1.
Time since boot: the 4 ms ticks of the timer counted at the length
.Xr adjtimex 2
gives each, with the place within the current tick added from the
reference that keeps the clock, the HPET, the PM timer, the TSC, under
KVM KVM's clock, or the 8254, at the same rate.
Where the kernel keeps the clock from the TSC or from KVM's clock page, as
.Xr vdso 7
describes, it is the TSC's count at the rate the kernel measured, or KVM's
clock, steered toward the ticks.
A reading is never earlier than the one before it, and setting the real
time does not move it.
.It Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW
ID 4.
Time since boot at the timer's own rate, which
.Xr adjtimex 2
does not slew, read as
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
is.
.It Dv CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID
ID 2.
The CPU time the timer has charged to the caller, user and system time
together, as
.Fn times
counts it.
.It Dv CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID
ID 3.
The calling thread's own CPU time; ID 2 reads that of all the process's
threads, those that have ended included.
.It Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME
ID 7.
The same time since boot as
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
since the machine is never suspended.
.It Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE
ID 6.
Time since boot at the last tick, without the place within it.
It moves only when the kernel counts a tick.
It is the time of the ticks counted, which
.Fn times
gives in its own units, plus the part of a tick, under one, that a slew of
the clock
.Pq Xr adjtimex 2
has carried.
.It Dv CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE
ID 5.
Real time at the last tick, without the place within it, so
never later than
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
read at the same moment.
.El
.Pp
A negative clock ID names a CPU clock by process or thread ID, in the
encoding
.Fn clock_getcpuclockid
returns: the bitwise complement of the ID shifted left three places, bit 2
set for a thread, and in the low two bits the time read, 1 for user time
alone and 0 or 2 for user and system time together.
An ID of 0 in it names the caller.
A process clock may name any process that has not been reaped, by its
process ID; a thread clock may name any thread of the caller's process.
.Pp
.Fn clock_getres
stores a clock's resolution in
.Fa res
unless it is null: for
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW
and
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME ,
one count of the reference, rounded up to a nanosecond, and 1 microsecond
with the 8254; and the 4 ms tick for every other clock, the coarse and CPU
clocks.
Each clock advances in steps of its resolution.
.Pp
The x86-64 kernel's two vDSOs serve coarse clocks and
.Fn __vdso_time
from the data page whatever clock source the kernel uses.
Fine readings there need the TSC or a stable KVM clock page and fall
back to the system call without one.
Musl implements its
.Fn time
through a fine
.Fn clock_gettime
reading.
See
.Xr vdso 7 .
The line
.Li clock_calls
of
.Pa /proc/stat
counts the system calls of the clock.
.Pp
.Fn times
returns the monotonic tick count in units of 1/100 second, as a signed
32-bit value that goes negative after about 249 days.
If
.Fa buf
is nonnull, it receives the CPU time of the caller's process, all its
threads', in the same units:
.Va tms_utime
and
.Va tms_stime
are the ticks the timer charged to its threads in user mode and in the
kernel, and
.Va tms_cutime
and
.Va tms_cstime
are the same two totals for the children the process has reaped with
.Xr wait 2 ,
each with the children those children reaped.
The timer charges each tick to the thread it interrupts, as user time
when that thread was in its own code and as system time when it was in
the kernel, and a tick that finds the processor idle to no thread.
The times are therefore samples: a process that runs only between ticks
is charged nothing for that time, as on a Linux kernel built with tick-based
accounting.
They start at zero in a child of
.Xr fork 2
and are kept across
.Xr execve 2 .
A null
.Fa buf
is allowed.
.Ss File timestamps
.Fn utimensat
sets the access and modification times of
.Fa path .
On ext2, a 256-byte inode keeps the nanoseconds and clamps seconds to
1901-12-13 through 2446-05-10; a 128-byte inode clamps them to the
signed 32-bit range, through 2038-01-19, and discards fractional seconds.
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
A relative path is resolved against the current working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD ,
or against the directory referred to by
.Fa dirfd
otherwise.
A path that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
names a directory: a final symbolic link is followed even under
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW ,
and anything but a directory returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux.
.Pp
A null
.Fa path
names the file
.Fa dirfd
refers to; GNU libc's and musl's
.Fn futimens
are this call.
It takes no flags and returns
.Er EINVAL
for any; with
.Dv AT_FDCWD
it returns
.Er EFAULT .
With
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
an empty
.Fa path
names the file
.Fa dirfd
refers to, or the current working directory for
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
A descriptor of a named FIFO, or of a device opened through a device node
of tmpfs or ext2, sets that node's times.
A descriptor on an anonymous pipe or a socket sets the times the object
keeps, as Linux sets those of its pipefs or sockfs inode, by the same
permission rules.
A descriptor on a file or directory of
.Pa /proc
or
.Pa /sys ,
or on a device of
.Pa /dev ,
sets times as the path does.
A descriptor on a terminal has no stored times and returns
.Er EROFS ;
Linux sets the times of such a file.
A descriptor that is not open returns
.Er EBADF
first, then a bad
.Va tv_nsec
returns
.Er EINVAL ,
then a read-only mount the descriptor was opened through
.Er EROFS ,
as Linux checks them.
A followed
.Pa /proc/PID/fd/N
sets the times of the file its descriptor is open on, judged as that
descriptor is, once the lookup's checks of the link have passed;
with
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
the lookup's search of
.Pa /proc/PID/fd
and the descriptor open come before every other check.
.Pp
The
.Fa times
argument points to two timespecs: access time first, modification time second.
A null pointer sets both to the current time.
For either element, a
.Va tv_nsec
of
.Dv UTIME_NOW
selects the current time;
.Dv UTIME_OMIT
leaves that timestamp unchanged.
When both elements specify
.Dv UTIME_OMIT ,
neither timestamp nor the change time is changed, and the call returns 0
before it looks at the path, the descriptor or the flags, as Linux does.
.Pp
The file's owner, judged by filesystem user ID, or a caller with
.Dv CAP_FOWNER
over its owner may set either timestamp.
On vfat and exfat, so may a caller in the file's group when the mount's
.Ql allow_utime
has 0020, and any other caller when it has 0002; see
.Xr mount 2 .
Write permission also suffices when setting both timestamps to the current time.
Omitting both timestamps requires neither.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
argument may have
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW ,
which changes a symbolic link's own timestamps instead of following it,
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH .
Other bits return
.Er EINVAL .
A
.Va tv_nsec
other than
.Dv UTIME_NOW ,
.Dv UTIME_OMIT
or 0 to 999999999 returns
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
A read-only mount or file system, the halted KRFFS root among them,
returns
.Er EROFS
before the ownership and permission checks, as Linux takes the mount
for writing first.
.Pp
Timestamp changes are supported on KRFFS, ext2, FAT32, exFAT, tmpfs,
debugfs, mqueue and pstore,
subject to the file system's stored precision.
On FAT32 and exFAT, a successful request on the volume's root
directory leaves its timestamps unchanged.
The root directory of KRFFS, the boot root's and a mounted volume's,
stores them under the checks above, as any node does.
A node of
.Pa /proc ,
.Pa /sys
or
.Pa /dev
takes times by the same permission rules; a node of a process or of
.Pa /proc/sys
keeps none, and any other keeps them, which its stat then gives, as
Linux's kernfs, proc_dir_entry and devtmpfs inodes keep them.
A terminal under
.Pa /dev/pts
returns
.Er EROFS
unless both timestamps are omitted.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 78 for
.Fn gettimeofday ,
265 for
.Fn clock_gettime ,
266 for
.Fn clock_getres ,
43 for
.Fn times ,
and 320 for
.Fn utimensat .
The time structures in this kernel interface have 32-bit seconds fields.
.Fn times
fills four 32-bit counts; for an i386 program on the x86-64 kernel, which
.Xr ia32 7
describes, each is cut to 32 bits, and so is the tick count it returns.
The signed 32-bit seconds field overflows in 2038 for real time and after
about 68 years since boot for monotonic time.
.Pp
musl and glibc first try
.Fn clock_gettime64
.Pq system call 403 ,
which reads the same clock into a timespec with 64-bit fields, as
.Fn clock_getres_time64
.Pq system call 406
reads the resolution; their seconds are those of the 32-bit calls.
musl calls system call 266 for
.Fn clock_getres
directly.
.Pp
.Fn utimensat_time64
.Pq system call 412
is
.Fn utimensat
with timespecs of 64-bit fields, so that a time past 2038 can be set.
Of each
.Va tv_nsec
only the low 32 bits are read, as on Linux i386, since GNU libc leaves the
high half as padding.
GNU libc tries it first for every
.Fn utimensat
and
.Fn futimens ;
musl makes it only for a time whose seconds do not fit in 32 bits.
.Pp
The x86-64 kernel also runs, for a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
which
.Xr ia32 7
describes, i386's
.Nm utime
(30), which takes a path and a pointer to two 32-bit seconds, the access
and the modification time;
.Nm utimes
(271), which takes a path and two timevals of 32-bit fields; and
.Nm futimesat
(299), which takes a directory descriptor before them.
A null pointer sets both times to the current time, and a microsecond
count outside 0 to 999999 is
.Er EINVAL .
musl implements
.Fn gettimeofday
through
.Fn clock_gettime ;
it accepts a null
.Fa tv
and returns 0.
The raw
.Fn gettimeofday
system call takes a null
.Fa tv
and returns 0, as Linux's does.
.Nm time
is system call 13; musl implements
.Fn time
through
.Fn clock_gettime .
.Pp
On x86-64 the clock calls take timespecs and timevals of 64-bit fields,
and there are no time64 forms and no
.Nm stime :
.Nm gettimeofday
is 96,
.Nm settimeofday
164,
.Nm time
201, which glibc calls for
.Fn time ,
.Nm clock_gettime
228,
.Nm clock_settime
227,
.Nm clock_getres
229 and
.Nm clock_nanosleep
230.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn gettimeofday ,
.Fn clock_gettime ,
.Fn clock_getres ,
and
.Fn utimensat
return 0 on success.
.Fn times
returns the tick count.
Kernel failures return a negative error number; the system call wrappers
return -1 and set
.Va errno ,
except musl's
.Fn times ,
which returns the kernel's result as it is: an invalid buffer gives the
raw -14 of
.Er EFAULT .
musl's
.Fn gettimeofday
returns 0 and writes its result in user space.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/timeprobe.c
reads
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
twice and checks that the second result is not earlier than the first.
.Pa tests/miscprobe.c
checks that no time
.Fn times
reports is negative or more than the ticks since boot, and
.Pa tests/musl/cputimemusl.c
checks that a loop in user mode gains user time, a loop of reads gains
system time, and a reaped child's time is added to the children's totals.
.Pa tests/musl/clockidmusl.c
reads every clock and its resolution, and a child's CPU clock through
.Fn clock_getcpuclockid .
.Pa tests/musl/statmusl.c
sets a file's times before 1970 and past 2^31 and 2^32 seconds, through
its path and through a descriptor, and reads them back with
.Fn stat
and
.Fn fstat .
.Pa tests/musl/fdcallmusl.c
sets a file's times through a null path and through an empty one with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
and checks the refusals of a null path.
.Sh ERRORS
The kernel handlers return the following errors:
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
For
.Fn utimensat ,
an ancestor directory denies search permission, or a caller that is
neither the owner nor privileged tries to set both times to the current
time without write permission.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fn utimensat
was given an invalid directory descriptor for a relative path, or the
descriptor refers to a removed KRFFS boot-root directory; or, with a null
path or an empty one and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
the descriptor is invalid or refers to a KRFFS boot-root file that is gone;
or, with a null path, the descriptor was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The
.Fn utimensat
path enters a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A required buffer cannot be read or written.
The raw
.Fn clock_gettime
timespec is null, a nonnull
.Fn gettimeofday
timeval or timezone cannot be written, a nonnull
.Fn settimeofday
timeval or timezone cannot be read, or a nonnull
.Fn clock_getres
timespec cannot be written.
For
.Fn utimensat ,
the path is null with
.Dv AT_FDCWD ,
or there is no current process.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn clock_settime
was given a clock other than
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
or a time-setting call a time before 1970, before the time since boot or
past the year 2232, or a fraction outside a second;
or
.Fn settimeofday
a time zone more than 900 minutes either side of UTC;
or
.Fa clockid
is 8 or greater, including Linux's alarm clocks and
.Dv CLOCK_TAI ,
a clock of a descriptor, a CPU clock of no process that has not been
reaped, a process CPU clock named by an ID that is not a process ID, or a
thread CPU clock of another process; or
.Fn utimensat
has flag bits other than
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
any flag with a null path, or a
.Va tv_nsec
out of range.
The clock is checked before the timespec is written.
.It Bq Er EIO
For
.Fn utimensat ,
the backing device has been removed, or a file-system read or metadata
write fails.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
The
.Fn utimensat
path requires more than 40 symbolic links to be followed.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The
.Fn utimensat
path, a component, a reconstructed directory path, or an expanded
symbolic-link path exceeds the resolver's buffer limit.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The
.Fn utimensat
path is empty without
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
or a component does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The file system cannot allocate a node while resolving the
.Fn utimensat
path, or the kernel has no page to copy the path into.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A component of the
.Fn utimensat
path is not a directory, a descriptor used as the base of a relative
path is not a directory, or the path ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
and reaches something else.
.It Bq Er EPERM
A caller that is not privileged sets the real time or the time zone, or
calls
.Fn settimeofday
with neither; or a caller that is
neither the owner nor privileged, nor admitted by a FAT mount's
.Ql allow_utime ,
requests a timestamp change other than
setting both times to the current time; or the file is immutable or
append-only, where Linux lets an append-only file have its times set to
the current time.
.It Bq Er EROFS
.Fn utimensat
targets a terminal under
.Pa /dev/pts ,
or a descriptor on a terminal, or the file's mount, or the mount a descriptor
was opened through, or its file system is read-only, or the file system
has no timestamp-setting operation.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr wait 2 ,
.Xr vdso 7
